1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,827
Here is a ball, bouncing. Each bounce is a little lower than the last, because some of

2
00:00:05,827 --> 00:00:07,656
its energy turns into heat.

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:14,800
Now play it backwards. The ball bounces higher and higher, and you know at once something

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,537
is wrong. That is the arrow of time.

5
00:00:17,933 --> 00:00:20,397
Physicists turn this into a measurement.

6
00:00:20,397 --> 00:00:24,893
The more wrong the backwards film looks, the more energy is being wasted.

7
00:00:25,267 --> 00:00:30,992
Tiny machines, like the motors in your cells, only show you a few jumps, and the waits

8
00:00:30,992 --> 00:00:34,387
between them. Can the waits alone reveal the waste?

9
00:00:34,767 --> 00:00:40,380
A published paper proposed a waiting-time test for machines on a repeating schedule,

10
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:45,927
and conjectured, meaning guessed without proof, that it never overstates the waste.

11
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:51,620
We will build a machine that wastes nothing, and yet the test says it does.

12
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,316
Pause, and ask: how could that happen?

13
00:00:55,300 --> 00:01:00,589
Here is the smallest machine we can build: three squares in a triangle, and a counter

14
00:01:00,589 --> 00:01:02,020
that hops between them.

15
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:09,017
Time passes in tiny ticks. Each tick, roll a die for every line out of the counter's

16
00:01:09,017 --> 00:01:12,168
square. If it says go, the counter hops.

17
00:01:12,567 --> 00:01:17,071
Each line has a rate: its average number of hops per unit of time.

18
00:01:17,071 --> 00:01:20,415
A bigger rate is a wider door. More faces say go.

19
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:25,873
Start simple. Every rate is one, in both directions.

20
00:01:25,873 --> 00:01:28,312
Watch the counter wander.

21
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:33,402
Over a long game, it spends a third of its time on each square.

22
00:01:33,402 --> 00:01:36,164
Those are the squares' probabilities.

23
00:01:36,828 --> 00:01:43,206
Now think about traffic: the chance of being on one, times the rate from one to two.

24
00:01:43,206 --> 00:01:45,180
Here, one third times one.

25
00:01:45,561 --> 00:01:48,926
Traffic back, from two to one, is the same.

26
00:01:48,926 --> 00:01:54,561
Every line balances both ways. That is detailed balance, or equilibrium.

27
00:01:54,961 --> 00:01:57,442
Played backwards, nothing looks wrong.

28
00:01:57,442 --> 00:02:02,209
A hop backwards is just a hop the other way, and both are equally common.

29
00:02:02,594 --> 00:02:08,954
Now push the counter clockwise: rate two one way round, and rate one the other way.

30
00:02:09,328 --> 00:02:14,707
Each square still holds a third, but every line carries twice the traffic clockwise.

31
00:02:14,707 --> 00:02:19,510
Backwards, the counter would mostly circle the wrong way: an arrow of time.

32
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:21,880
Like a motor, this game burns energy.

33
00:02:22,556 --> 00:02:27,236
That waste is measured by a number called the entropy production.

34
00:02:27,236 --> 00:02:28,820
We will call it sigma.

35
00:02:29,190 --> 00:02:33,862
We need one tool, the logarithm. The log of one is zero.

36
00:02:33,862 --> 00:02:37,950
It is positive above one, and negative below one.

37
00:02:38,323 --> 00:02:44,144
For every line, in each direction, multiply the traffic by the log of traffic this way

38
00:02:44,144 --> 00:02:46,987
over traffic that way. Then add it all up.

39
00:02:47,356 --> 00:02:52,691
For the clockwise game, the total is log two, about zero point seven.

40
00:02:52,691 --> 00:02:58,876
For the balanced game, every ratio is one, every log is zero, and sigma is zero.

41
00:02:59,256 --> 00:03:02,904
Balanced at every moment means no waste.

42
00:03:03,589 --> 00:03:09,339
Real machines are harder to watch. Suppose the line from one to three is hidden, so you

43
00:03:09,339 --> 00:03:11,389
only see hops on the other two.

44
00:03:11,789 --> 00:03:18,458
So you record pairs: a visible jump, the next one, and the wait between.

45
00:03:18,458 --> 00:03:21,701
Say, one to two, then two to three.

46
00:03:22,089 --> 00:03:27,472
Backwards, that becomes three to two, then two to one, with the same wait.

47
00:03:27,472 --> 00:03:31,401
With no arrow of time, both pairs show the same waits.

48
00:03:31,789 --> 00:03:34,563
Draw a waiting-time curve for each pair.

49
00:03:34,563 --> 00:03:38,101
In the balanced game, the two curves match exactly.

50
00:03:38,489 --> 00:03:44,511
The test adds up the mismatches between each pair's curve and its reverse's, into one

51
00:03:44,511 --> 00:03:47,345
number: sigma hat, an estimate of sigma.

52
00:03:47,722 --> 00:03:51,579
The whole film's backwards wrongness would equal sigma.

53
00:03:51,579 --> 00:03:55,015
Seeing only part, you would expect to catch less.

54
00:03:55,015 --> 00:03:58,522
So the hope is that sigma hat never exceeds sigma.

55
00:03:59,222 --> 00:04:02,534
Now drive the machine: doors on a timer.

56
00:04:02,922 --> 00:04:10,013
Let t be the angle of a clock hand. Door a, between one and two, has rate one plus epsilon

57
00:04:10,013 --> 00:04:16,789
times cosine t. Door b, between two and three, has rate one plus epsilon times sine t.

58
00:04:16,789 --> 00:04:19,074
The hidden line stays at one.

59
00:04:19,455 --> 00:04:24,514
Epsilon, between zero and one, sets how far the doors swing.

60
00:04:24,514 --> 00:04:25,863
We use one half.

61
00:04:26,255 --> 00:04:32,509
Door a is widest at the start of each turn, and door b a quarter turn later.

62
00:04:32,509 --> 00:04:34,319
A opens first, then b.

63
00:04:34,688 --> 00:04:41,771
In 2024, Maier and colleagues proposed such a test for driven machines, using only forward-schedule

64
00:04:41,771 --> 00:04:45,848
data, and conjectured that sigma hat never exceeds sigma.

65
00:04:46,222 --> 00:04:50,929
But a true backwards film would run the schedule backwards too.

66
00:04:50,929 --> 00:04:53,470
This test only reverses the jumps.

67
00:04:54,155 --> 00:05:01,259
First, the true waste. At every instant, each door is equally wide in both directions.

68
00:05:01,655 --> 00:05:07,596
So with a third on each square, traffic in matches traffic out, and the probabilities

69
00:05:07,596 --> 00:05:08,855
stay at one third.

70
00:05:09,255 --> 00:05:12,635
Every line is balanced at every moment.

71
00:05:12,635 --> 00:05:17,055
Sigma is exactly zero. This machine wastes nothing.

72
00:05:17,821 --> 00:05:25,389
Now the waits. A hop through door a is most likely when door a is wide, near the start

73
00:05:25,389 --> 00:05:26,269
of a turn.

74
00:05:26,655 --> 00:05:33,255
Just then, door b is opening. So the chance of the next hop, through door b, is rising.

75
00:05:33,655 --> 00:05:38,873
The reverse pair starts with a hop through door b, likely a quarter turn in.

76
00:05:38,873 --> 00:05:44,503
Then door a is closing, so the chance of the next hop, through door a, is falling.

77
00:05:44,888 --> 00:05:50,493
So the test reports an arrow of time. There is one, but it is the schedule's: door a

78
00:05:50,493 --> 00:05:53,096
opens before door b. That is not waste.

79
00:05:53,788 --> 00:06:00,316
Let's make it exact. Call the forward pair's curve f, and the reverse pair's curve g.

80
00:06:00,316 --> 00:06:02,236
Both start at height one.

81
00:06:02,621 --> 00:06:08,969
Compare their starting slopes. Write a prime for how fast door a is changing, and angle

82
00:06:08,969 --> 00:06:11,741
brackets for an average over one turn.

83
00:06:12,121 --> 00:06:17,866
Each slope has two parts. The second, from leaving square two through either door, is

84
00:06:17,866 --> 00:06:20,569
the same for both curves, so it cancels.

85
00:06:20,955 --> 00:06:27,219
What remains is the average of a times b prime, minus b times a prime.

86
00:06:27,588 --> 00:06:34,847
Put in our doors. The slope of sine follows cosine, and the slope of cosine follows minus

87
00:06:34,847 --> 00:06:36,804
sine. Then multiply out.

88
00:06:37,188 --> 00:06:41,574
By Pythagoras, cosine squared plus sine squared is one.

89
00:06:41,574 --> 00:06:46,279
And over a full turn, cosine and sine each average to zero.

90
00:06:46,279 --> 00:06:49,548
What survives is exactly epsilon squared.

91
00:06:49,921 --> 00:06:56,466
With epsilon one half, the slopes are minus two point one two five and minus two point

92
00:06:56,466 --> 00:06:59,433
three seven five: a gap of one quarter.

93
00:06:59,433 --> 00:07:05,521
So f starts above g. An exact program, using fractions, confirms every identity.

94
00:07:06,219 --> 00:07:09,753
Here is f minus g, for epsilon one half.

95
00:07:09,753 --> 00:07:13,375
It starts at zero with slope one quarter.

96
00:07:13,375 --> 00:07:16,467
Later, g catches up, and overtakes.

97
00:07:16,853 --> 00:07:23,670
Can the differences cancel? No. Each pair is scored with its reverse as f minus g, times

98
00:07:23,670 --> 00:07:28,162
the log of f over g. Where f is bigger, both are positive.

99
00:07:28,162 --> 00:07:34,205
Where g is bigger, both are negative, and negative times negative is positive.

100
00:07:34,586 --> 00:07:39,886
All four visible jumps happen equally often, so this works for every pair.

101
00:07:39,886 --> 00:07:45,973
In fact, sigma hat is four thirds times a standard measure of mismatch, which is zero

102
00:07:45,973 --> 00:07:48,050
only when nothing mismatches.

103
00:07:48,419 --> 00:07:53,118
So sigma hat is positive, and the paper proves it is finite.

104
00:07:53,118 --> 00:07:59,148
But sigma is exactly zero. The conjecture said sigma hat never exceeds sigma.

105
00:07:59,148 --> 00:08:02,123
Here it does. The conjecture is false.

106
00:08:02,519 --> 00:08:08,806
This happens for every epsilon between zero and one, with smooth rates that never reach

107
00:08:08,806 --> 00:08:09,167
zero.

108
00:08:09,853 --> 00:08:16,021
So what does this kill? One thing: the forward-only conjecture, in its general form.

109
00:08:16,419 --> 00:08:20,989
The same paper's theorem using backwards-schedule data still stands.

110
00:08:20,989 --> 00:08:24,483
Reversing the jumps is not reversing the whole film.

111
00:08:24,853 --> 00:08:27,370
Waiting-time false alarms are not new.

112
00:08:27,370 --> 00:08:33,861
Hartich and Godec found one at equilibrium, and Harunari, Fiore and Barato used backwards-schedule

113
00:08:33,861 --> 00:08:38,101
data. What is new is an exact counterexample to this conjecture.

114
00:08:38,486 --> 00:08:43,339
Nothing is claimed about the energy used by the device turning the doors.

115
00:08:43,339 --> 00:08:45,134
That is outside this sigma.

116
00:08:45,519 --> 00:08:50,164
How sure is this? The proof is fully written, and needs no simulation.

117
00:08:50,164 --> 00:08:55,339
A computer checked the algebra exactly, and internal reviews checked the rest.

118
00:08:55,339 --> 00:08:59,319
There is no formal machine proof or outside peer review yet.

119
00:09:00,019 --> 00:09:07,390
Three takeaways. One: entropy production measures waste, and this machine's is exactly

120
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:07,819
zero.

121
00:09:08,186 --> 00:09:14,690
Two: its doors open in a fixed order, so a jump pair and its reverse wait differently.

122
00:09:15,086 --> 00:09:20,959
Three: a test that keeps the schedule running forwards mistakes that order for waste.

123
00:09:20,959 --> 00:09:23,102
So the conjectured bound fails.

124
00:09:23,486 --> 00:09:29,053
Still unknown: whether a narrower forward-only test is safe, with extra rules on how

125
00:09:29,053 --> 00:09:32,764
the rates may change, and whether this was found before.

126
00:09:32,764 --> 00:09:36,542
The search of earlier work was careful, but not complete.

127
00:09:36,919 --> 00:09:39,871
Thanks for watching.
